// Day 12: Trace a Safe Links click back to the email that carried it
//
// What it looks for: messages from one external sender to one recipient (EmailEvents), and
// that recipient's Safe Links click rows (UrlClickEvents). Run the two queries separately and
// compare NetworkMessageId. Safe Links rewrites a link at delivery and logs the click.
//
// Why it matters: the same NetworkMessageId ties a click, an Explorer entry, and a user report
// to one message. IsClickedThrough means the user clicked past a warning page, not that they
// clicked the link.
//
// Result in my lab: two messages from the test sender, the 11:47 AM one with UrlCount 0 and the
// 11:50 AM one with UrlCount 1. One UrlClickEvents row, ActionType ClickAllowed for
// https://google.com/, IsClickedThrough 0, whose NetworkMessageId matches the 11:50 AM email.
// UrlClickEvents was empty right after the click and filled in later because of ingestion delay.
EmailEvents
| where RecipientEmailAddress contains "richard"
| where SenderFromAddress =~ "phishingmydfir@gmail.com"
| project Timestamp, UrlCount, Subject, SenderFromAddress, RecipientEmailAddress, DeliveryAction, DeliveryLocation, NetworkMessageId
| sort by Timestamp asc

// --- Second query: run it separately. Advanced hunting shows only the last result in a tab. ---
UrlClickEvents
| where AccountUpn startswith "richard.hale@"
| project Timestamp, AccountUpn, Url, ActionType, Workload, IsClickedThrough
